    39	---
    40	
    41	## Definition
    42	
    43	Let $A$ and $B$ be sets ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]] for the
    44	terminology).
    45	
    46	- $A$ and $B$ are **equinumerous**, written $A \approx B$, if there exists a
    47	  bijection $f : A \to B$.
    48	- $A$ is **dominated by** $B$, written $A \preceq B$, if there exists an
    49	  injection $f : A \to B$.
    50	- $A \prec B$ abbreviates: $A \preceq B$ and not $A \approx B$.
    51	
    52	## Remarks
    53	
    54	- **$\approx$ behaves like an equivalence relation.** It is reflexive
    55	  ($\mathrm{id}_A$ is a bijection), symmetric (the inverse of a bijection is a
    56	  bijection) and transitive (a composition of bijections is a bijection). The
    57	  careful statement is that these three properties hold for all sets, and that
    58	  $\approx$ restricted to any *set* of sets is an equivalence relation on that
    59	  set. It is not a relation on "the set of all sets", which does not exist; the
    60	  reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity statements are schemas about arbitrary
    61	  sets, which is all any argument below uses.
    62	
    63	- **$\preceq$ is reflexive and transitive**, for the same reasons, and
    64	  $A \approx B$ implies both $A \preceq B$ and $B \preceq A$. The converse, that
    65	  $A \preceq B$ and $B \preceq A$ together give $A \approx B$, is a theorem and
    66	  not a triviality: it is [[thm-schroder-bernstein]], and it is proved without
    67	  any use of choice.
    68	
    69	- **Subsets.** $A \subseteq B$ implies $A \preceq B$, since the inclusion map is
    70	  injective. The reverse fails badly for infinite sets: the successor map
    71	  $\sigma$ is a bijection $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N} \setminus \{0\}$, being
    72	  injective and never zero ([[thm-omega-is-peano-system]]) and hitting every
    73	  nonzero natural ([[lem-nat-nonzero-is-successor]]), so
    74	  $\mathbb{N} \approx \mathbb{N} \setminus \{0\}$ and a proper subset can be
    75	  equinumerous with the whole.
    76	
    77	- $\approx$ is the library's substitute for "has the same number of elements",
    78	  stated without introducing cardinal numbers. Everything on this page is phrased
    79	  with $\approx$, $\preceq$ and $\prec$ alone, so no theory of cardinals is
    80	  presupposed.

